Razer is known for its many PC gaming  peripherals but they've also dipped into  the console space with a few products  one of which is the Razer rise you it's  a ps4 controller similar to the Xbox one  elite with its extra triggers and better  grips there is a catch though this  controller is currently only available  in Sony EMEA markets which means if  you're in the US you'll have to import  it it also carries a hefty price tag at  a hundred and fifty British pounds which  converts to about 195 US dollars however  the riser is actually a significant  upgrade from the standard Dual Shock 4  but before we get into what it has to  offer  be sure to subscribe to our channel and  check out our review of scuff gaming's  ps4 controllers now let's get into what  it has to offer and break down the pros  and cons of the Razr rise you the risers  body is bulkier than the DualShock floor  but this can be an up side it's suitable  for users who have bigger hands and want  more to hold onto the rise you itself  weighs about 10 ounces for reference the  DualShock 4 weighs 7 point 4 ounces this  controller only works through a wired  connection and there is no wireless  option it's USB port has notches around  it and it only fits the package of USB  cable a cable with low profile housing  can fit here but if you happen to  misplace the cable you'd be out of luck  the face buttons feel just like mouse  clicks while this takes some getting  used to the distinct tactile feedback in  short travel distance make X square  circle and triangle much more responsive  the extra bumpers on top and extra  triggers behind the controller also feel  similar to mouse clicks all four buttons  have a short throw and tactile feel and  using them as effortless they're easy to  program as well simply hold down the  left mouse button on the bottom row on  the front press the trigger or bumper  you want to map then the button you want  it to act as the controller will then  Rumble if you don't want the extra  triggers in the way for certain games  you can  move them with the package screwdriver  l1 and r1 have a satisfying click and  more surface area compared to the  DualShock 4 which makes them much easier  to use pulling l2 and r2 is smooth and  light on the fingers and much more  consistent than the DualShock port  triggers trigger stops also shorten the  throw and can slightly improve quickness  another highlight of the Rio is the  fluidity of the analog sticks going back  to the DualShock for the stiffness of  the sticks was more apparent and precise  movements were a little bit more  difficult rubber grips come attached to  the sticks which we recommend keeping on  since they prevented our thumbs from  slipping the same type of grip is also  wrapped around the controller's handles  the d-pad on the ratio is stiff and it  takes considerable amount of force to  press we wouldn't recommend using it in  games that heavily rely on the d-pad  like fighting games injustice 2 and  Street Fighter 5 come to mind speaking  of games how well did rise use features  translate to the heat of battle well  like our other reviews we played through  overwatch titanfall 2 blood-borne and  horizon zero dawn extensively titanfall  2 was a joy to play with responsive  programmable buttons stringing wall runs  together and hopping around and being  able to look freely all at the same time  provided a significant advantage landing  shots from afar was easier with the  fluid analog sticks having the option to  map character abilities to the extra  buttons in overwatch was extremely  helpful constantly jumping without  having to give up aiming by using the  extra triggers help to stay nimble  during firefights mapping the ultimate  button to another trigger was clutch for  offensive characters that require aiming  for the ability to be effective while  you wouldn't necessarily buy an  expensive controller for single-player  games the Rajah's advantages made  blood-borne and horizon zero dawn more  enjoyable mapping the dodge action in  both games to a trigger allowed us to  keep the camera moving while avoiding  enemy attacks  overall the rise you sets itself apart  from other premium controllers since the  extra buttons feel tactile and are easy  to press it's a pleasure to use with its  responsive buttons grippy textures and  fluid analog sticks as much as I enjoyed  using the razor rise you I can only  recommend it if you don't mind spending  a huge amount of money on a controller  or jumping through hoops to get it in  the US you'll also want to pass on it if  Wireless is a must if you liked this  video give it a thumbs up and subscribe  to keep up with GameSpot don't forget to  check out my review of scuffed gaming  ps4 controllers and stay tuned for my  review of the Nikon revolution  controller  thanks for watching
 

